## Background

INSO provides safety analysis and incident reporting for NGO workers operating in high-risk environments. The website is the main entry point to this information, and to INSO’s role.

**My goal** was to redesign and implement a new website that shows the value of INSO’s work, gives different users a way in and makes trust and clarity the default experience.

The previous website was built by an external agency in 2015, on a bespoke CMS that had hardly changed since. In 2021 INSO turned ten, and the redesign was part of the celebrations.

The goals set for the redesign:

- One-click access to the three main sections: dashboards, registration and careers
- A smooth registration and contact experience
- Friendly on small devices and low bandwidth
- A content system that INSO staff from communications, IT and HR can manage, each with their own permissions
- Flexible enough to host external web applications

## At first glance, the problem seemed straightforward.

INSO needed better visibility among NGOs, and the website wasn’t effectively supporting recruitment.

But as the project unfolded, it became clear that these assumptions were not backed by data.

{% table %}
- Initial assumptions
- What we found
---
- NGOs lack awareness of INSO
- No data to confirm this
---
- Website underperforms for hiring
- No baseline to measure it
---
- A redesign would solve the issue
- No clearly defined user problems
{% /table %}

There were no analytics in place before the redesign.

> Decisions were expected to be data-informed, but no reliable data existed. Direction was largely top-down, while my role gradually expanded beyond design into implementation, planning, and coordination.

Within that context, I still tried to introduce structure where possible. I defined user groups, mapped their needs, and later initiated usability testing on my own.

## Research with what we had

The communications plan listed nine audiences by what INSO wanted them to do, know and feel. With no data on how they used the site, I grouped them by what they come to the website to do.

{% usertable %}
{% table %}
- Who
- Come to the site to
- Challenges
---
- **NGOs** · Field specialists staying informed on their region, subscribed or not yet
- Find the services INSO offers and how to sign up · Check the latest NGO incidents · Ask for tailored analysis
- Limited awareness of INSO and unclear understanding of its services and value · Difficulty accessing and understanding the safety and analysis products
---
- **Donors, UN and general public** · Senior stakeholders following INSO's countries and HQ analysis
- Understand INSO's impact on humanitarian safety · See what INSO has been up to · Get the data and analysis available
- Limited visibility into INSO's day-to-day operations and analysis beyond official reports
---
- **Job candidates** · Professionals looking for a job in the humanitarian field
- See what roles are open · Learn what it is like to work at INSO · Find out how to apply
- Lack of clarity on roles and working environment · Difficulty browsing opportunities, especially by location
---
- **INSO HR** · Colleagues managing job postings
- Post and manage vacancies
- Managing postings and tracking their performance across platforms
{% /table %}
{% /usertable %}

With no analytics in place, the only inputs were two internal surveys.

**Survey 1 – Perception:** 599 answers, 72% from NGOs, 62% had visited the website. {% sourceline text="From INSO's brand survey, 599 answers" /%}

![Aggregated survey results. This is one piece of the full survey answered by 599 different actors in the humanitarian sector.](../../assets/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-website-initial-survey-e1771236429678.jpg)

*Aggregated survey results. This is one piece of the full survey answered by 599 different actors in the humanitarian sector.*

The **most useful insight** came from recurring requests for clearer access to dashboards, maps, and analysis content.

**Survey 2 – Services:** Highlighted which services were most valued, particularly analysis reports (84%) and flash alerts (73%).

![INSO services popularity](../../assets/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/Website_Services_Survey-results.png)

*INSO services popularity*

While these inputs helped support certain decisions, most key decisions regarding content and structure were ultimately made top-down.

## Structuring ideas into task flows, sitemaps and wireframes

Ideation was not a formal phase of the project. Ideas gradually emerged through discussions with my line manager, and I organized them into cards to group and clarify user needs.

The affinity map below reflects this synthesis.

![Summary of ideation through affinity mapping exercises](../../assets/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/Ideation_affinity_map-2.jpg)

*Summary of ideation through affinity mapping exercises*

### Use case scenario

To structure the experience, I focused on key user journeys. One representative case:

How might a non-partner NGO planning an operation find relevant information and eventually register for full access?

![The diagram illustrates a simplified journey from public content to registration.](../../assets/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/task_analysis_diagram-2.jpg)

*The diagram illustrates a simplified journey from public content to registration.*

### Website sitemap

The structure was iteratively refined from an initial draft, with a focus on simplifying navigation, grouping related content, and making page naming more clear and accessible.

![INSO website sitemap (as of April 2022)](../../assets/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/website_sitemap-2.jpg)

*INSO website sitemap (as of April 2022)*

### Wireframing

I started with a mockup of the homepage with placeholders. With our Communications Manager, we then defined the form, tone and length of the content, to answer user needs.

{% scrollshot
   src="projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-website-home-mockup.jpg"
   alt="The homepage wireframe, from the top of the page to the footer."
   caption="The homepage wireframe served as a foundation for the rest of the website’s wireframing." /%}

## Visual style

The revamp required updating INSO’s visual style, particularly the colour palette and typography, which felt outdated and inconsistent. I reworked the palette to improve contrast and usability, while still keeping a clear link to INSO’s original branding.

The brief asked for six traits: clear, professional, reliable, discreet, informed and innovative.

This work later evolved into a more complete [brand guide](https://brand.ngosafety.org/) used internally by INSO.

![The website’s visual style guide](../../assets/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-website-styles.jpg)

*The website’s visual style guide*

## What changed?

I redesigned the homepage to better reflect what INSO does and make key information easier to understand and access.

The overall layout was also simplified to guide users more naturally through the content, starting with what INSO does before moving to updates and job opportunities.

1. More visual and less text-heavy content
1. Clearer introduction of INSO’s mission and role
1. Key figures added to give a sense of scale
1. Easier access to reports and data

![INSO Website – former & current homepage design comparison](../../assets/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/old-new-site-homepage-comparison.jpg)

*INSO Website – former & current homepage design comparison*

Explore the full website: [**NGOsafety.org**](https://ngosafety.org/)

## Usability testing

I ran task-based usability testing on the staging site with 12 participants, mostly INSO colleagues from different departments, with different levels of tech savviness. They thought aloud on 9 tasks, and each attempt was rated: completed at the first attempt, completed with difficulties, or not completed.

Each colleague took 2 or 3 of these tasks:

- Find INSO's partners, countries and sectors
- Check if INSO works in a given country
- Find the latest conflict trends
- Find articles on a topic through search and tags
- Find and download a past report (two variants)
- Learn about a service as an NGO
- Register for a service
- Check if INSO mentions a donor's contribution

{% attempts
   rows="Finding information|4|8|4|0;Finding a past report|2|1|3|4;Services, registration and donors|3|4|4|3" /%}

{% iconblock
   icon=""
   source="From task-based usability sessions on the staging site"
   result=false
   note=false %}
**Finding information worked. Finding a past report and registering did not.** Of 12 attempts at finding information, none failed. Of 8 attempts at finding a report, 4 did.
{% /iconblock %}

In the written feedback, 4 of 9 liked the modern look and 3 of 9 the top navigation.

In the second round, the two colleagues who had failed to find a report {% mark text="completed the task" color="green" /%}, after the changes below.

{% sequence
   images="projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-website-ut-record-blurred.jpg, projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-website-ut-feedback-blurred.jpg"
   alts="The task observations: one row per task, one column per colleague (names blurred), each attempt marked green, orange or red.|The open feedback: what worked well and what needs improvement, one column per colleague (names blurred)."
   captions="Usability testing report – Task based session|Usability testing – short interview + open feedback session"
   frame=""
   thumbs=false
   landscape=true /%}

### Iterate with an action plan

I organised the findings into an action plan, addressing some issues first and deprioritising others.

{% table %}
- Identified problem
- Raised by
- What changed
---
- Report categories unclear, archive hard to reach
- 7 of 12
- All report categories in one filter, shorter page intro, categories next to the report grid, tested in a second round
---
- Long pages, spacing and animation didn't invite exploring
- 6 of 11
- Text blocks limited to 2 paragraphs, less padding on small screens, reveal animation triggered earlier
---
- Registration not clear on the homepage, hard to find elsewhere, and partner support read as being for NGOs already supported
- 6 of 11
- "Register here" in the hero, login/register back in the main navigation, shortcut in Contact, "How to register?" in the FAQ
---
- "Analysis hub" didn't say "reports"
- 5 of 11
- Renamed to "Analysis reports", "Data & analysis" to "Data & reports"
---
- Homepage too long after the impact section
- 4 of 11
- The two dashboard blocks combined, "more news" removed
---
- Slugs not read, only the big titles
- 4 of 11
- Big titles introduce each section
{% /table %}

![Post UT action plan](../../assets/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-website-ut-action-plan-cropped.jpg)

*Post UT action plan*

## Build / Implementation

### Frontend: Building the custom theme

I structured the website using a combination of reusable components and flexible templates, allowing different types of content to be built consistently across pages.

{% sequence
   images="projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-website-editable-layout-components-02.png, projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-website-component-layout-01.png"
   alts="Example of editable components (Navigation, Hero section text, relative content navigation, 2 columns title/text)|Ordered blocks were used to communicate information in different formats (text, data figure, visual & statement) and in a specific order."
   captions="Example of editable components (Navigation, Hero section text, relative content navigation, 2 columns title/text)|Ordered blocks were used to communicate information in different formats (text, data figure, visual & statement) and in a specific order."
   frame=""
   thumbs=false
   landscape=true /%}

Once the structure was defined, I implemented the design in plain HTML, SCSS, and JavaScript, focusing on responsiveness, interaction, and long-term maintainability.

{% video
   src="/media/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-nav-menu-desktop.mp4"
   alt="Desktop navigation with multi-level interactions and hover states"
   portrait=false
   device="" /%}

{% video
   src="/media/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-site-mobile-nav-clean.mp4"
   alt="Mobile navigation with nested structure and touch interactions"
   portrait=true
   webm="/media/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-site-mobile-nav-clean.webm"
   poster="/media/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-site-mobile-nav-poster.jpg"
   device="phone" /%}

### Customisation work in the content management system (CMS)

Since I developed the website on a custom WordPress theme, I used ACF (Advanced Custom Fields) to build the content management layer. This way, non-technical teams can manage content independently, with different permissions per department.

{% sequence
   images="projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-web-acf.png, projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-web-vacancies-management-wp-blurred.png, projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-web-reusable-components-structure-code.png"
   alts="Flexible page builder using ACF, allowing non-technical users to compose pages using predefined components.|Custom vacancies management system with status indicators and expiration logic, enabling HR to manage job listings directly.|Example of reusable component structure in PHP, powering the modular page builder and dynamic layouts."
   captions="Flexible page builder using ACF, allowing non-technical users to compose pages using predefined components.|Custom vacancies management system with status indicators and expiration logic, enabling HR to manage job listings directly.|Example of reusable component structure in PHP, powering the modular page builder and dynamic layouts."
   frame=""
   thumbs=false
   landscape=true /%}

## Outcomes

The new design led to noticeable improvements in engagement and navigation across the website.

I set up analytics on the former site during the project to establish a baseline, allowing a direct comparison with the updated version.

Two four-week windows, one on the former site and one right after the launch:

{% table %}
- Metric
- Former site
- New site
---
- Visitors
- 7,761
- 9,089
---
- Bounce rate
- 62%
- 59%
---
- Pages per session
- 1.99
- 2.17
---
- Session duration
- 1:52
- 2:11
---
- Time on the homepage
- 1:35
- 2:57
---
- Time on the vacancies page (Careers on the former site)
- 1:31
- 2:39
---
- Devices
- 57% desktop, 43% mobile
- 57% desktop, 43% mobile
{% /table %}

Where we work, a page that didn't exist on the former site, held attention for 2:01.

The results below show how user interaction improved across key metrics.

![Website evaluation through analytics](../../assets/projects/ngosafety-website-redesign/inso-website-analytics-comparison.jpg)

*Website evaluation through analytics*

**What I can't say.** The two windows are different months and the second is the first month after launch, so I can't separate the design from the season or the novelty. The mobile share stayed at 43%, which is why small devices and low bandwidth were a goal from the start.

## Takeaway

**Create the evidence you're missing**

There was no data, so I set up analytics on the former site mid-project to get a baseline, and ran usability testing on my own initiative. They gave the decisions something to stand on, and the results something to be compared with.
